  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through Sunday)\nIssued at 1250 PM MDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\nThe flow aloft turns more southwesterly into Wednesday, but stays\ndry with modest speeds (20-25 kt mean in the 500-700 mb layer). \nPWATs would stay in a 0.15 to 0.3 inch range across the \nnorthwestern half of NM Wednesday with the southeastern half \nstruggling to hold onto 0.4 to 0.7 inch), and no sufficient lift\nwould be present for convection.\n\nThe southwesterly flow aloft will back ever so slightly into\nThursday, drawing in subtle rises in moisture and instability to \nthe southern zones. The added kicker is another shortwave trough \nlifting north northeastward out of Chihuahua. Showers and \nthunderstorms will develop south of I-40 Thursday afternoon, \nexpanding northward overnight. The deepest moisture (PWATs of 0.8 \nto 0.9 inch) looks to primarily stay along our southern tier of \ncounties and farther south with more diffuse or modified moisture \nfarther north. The shortwave will lift toward the eastern border\nof NM and west TX on Friday, carrying associated precipitation\nwith it while some subsidence is introduced into central areas of\nthe state. Farther west, another spoke of vorticity will be \narriving into AZ and western NM Friday afternoon, potentially \nleading to another wave of scattered showers and thunderstorms \nthat would then translate eastward across the state Friday night.\nDeterministic models have timing differences with the evolution of\nthis Thursday-Friday time frame, so confidence is not overly high.\n\nThis would likely lead to some subsidence that would limit shower\nand storm activity into Saturday, but blended guidance retains\nsome isolated to scattered coverage. By Saturday, the upstream\nfocus will certainly be the Pacific Northwestern states where an\ninland-bound upper low will be arriving while a moist trough\ninches closer to the Baja peninsula via the subtropical jet. This\nlooks to drag ample subtropical moisture into NM Sunday into\nMonday with a somewhat phased trough axis between the two features\ncrossing NM. Much uncertainty still exists on the finer details,\nbut a transient passage of scattered to numerous showers and\nthunderstorms (and high elevation snow) looks to cross with \nincreases in wind and reductions in temperatures.",
